(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-06"] = {"10.1":{"s":"Matter exists as a solid, a liquid or a gas. Which state it takes depends on a balance between two things: the attraction between the particles, which pulls them together, and their thermal energy, which moves them apart. Cooling or raising the pressure favours the denser states, and heating favours the gas. This chapter deals mainly with gases and liquids: their forces, laws and behaviour."},"10.2":{"s":"Molecules attract each other by intermolecular forces, which are much weaker than the bonds inside molecules. Dispersion (London) forces act between all molecules and rise with the size of the molecule. Dipole-dipole forces act between polar molecules. A hydrogen bond is a specially strong attraction in molecules where hydrogen is bonded to N, O or F. These forces explain boiling points and the state of a substance.","w":["Stronger intermolecular forces mean a higher boiling point: water boils higher than H₂S because of hydrogen bonding.","Intermolecular forces (between molecules) are different from covalent bonds (inside molecules)."]},"10.3":{"s":"Gases have a few typical properties. They have no fixed shape or volume and fill the whole container. They can be compressed easily, because the molecules are far apart. They mix completely with one another and diffuse quickly. Their density is low, and they exert pressure on the walls of the container. Pressure, volume, temperature and the amount of gas are the four variables that describe a gas."},"10.4":{"s":"The gas laws link pressure, volume, temperature and amount. Boyle's law: at constant temperature, the volume of a fixed amount of gas is inversely proportional to its pressure. Charles's law: at constant pressure, the volume is directly proportional to the temperature in kelvin. Gay-Lussac's law relates pressure and temperature at constant volume. Avogadro's law relates volume and the amount of gas.","f":[{"x":"P*V = k","n":"Boyle's law: constant temperature and amount of gas. For two states: P_1*V_1 = P_2*V_2.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 10.4, p.143"},{"x":"V/T = k","n":"Charles's law: constant pressure and amount of gas, T in kelvin. For two states: V_1/T_1 = V_2/T_2.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 10.4, p.144"}],"w":["Always put temperature in kelvin: T (K) = t (°C) + 273.15. A Celsius value in a gas law gives a wrong answer.","Boyle: volume falls as pressure rises. Charles: volume rises as temperature rises."]},"10.5":{"s":"Combining Boyle's, Charles's and Avogadro's laws gives the ideal gas equation, which relates all four variables of a gas. A gas that obeys it at all conditions is called an ideal gas. The constant R is the same for every gas. Its value depends on the units used for pressure and volume. The equation also gives the molar mass of a gas from its density, and Dalton's law of partial pressures follows from it.","f":[{"x":"P*V = n*R*T","n":"Ideal gas equation: n = moles, T in kelvin, R = gas constant.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 10.5, p.147"},{"x":"R = 8.314 J K^-1 mol^-1","n":"R in SI units. In litre atmosphere: R = 0.08206 L atm K^-1 mol^-1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 10.5, p.147"},{"x":"M = d*R*T/P","n":"Molar mass M of a gas from its density d, pressure P and temperature T.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 10.5, p.149"},{"x":"P_total = P_1 + P_2 + P_3 + ...","n":"Dalton's law: total pressure of a mixture of non-reacting gases at constant V and T is the sum of the partial pressures.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 10.5, p.149"}],"w":["Match R to the units of P and V: 8.314 with pascal and m³, 0.08206 with atm and litre.","Dalton's law is for gases that do not react with each other."]},"10.6":{"s":"The kinetic molecular theory explains gas behaviour by a model. A gas is made of very many tiny molecules in constant random motion. They are so small compared with the distances between them that their own volume can be ignored. There are no attractive or repulsive forces between them, and their collisions are elastic. The average kinetic energy of the molecules depends only on the temperature.","w":["Average kinetic energy is proportional to the absolute temperature, and is the same for all gases at one temperature."]},"10.7":{"s":"Real gases obey the ideal gas equation only at high temperature and low pressure. At high pressure or low temperature they deviate, because the molecules have a real volume and attract each other. The compressibility factor Z measures this: Z is 1 for an ideal gas, more than 1 when the gas is hard to compress, and less than 1 when attractions help compress it. Below the critical temperature a gas can be liquefied.","f":[{"x":"Z = P*V/(n*R*T)","n":"Compressibility factor. Z = 1 for an ideal gas; Z > 1 positive deviation; Z < 1 negative deviation.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 10.7, p.153"}],"w":["A gas can be liquefied only below its critical temperature; above it, pressure alone cannot make it a liquid.","Z = 1 means ideal behaviour. At very high pressure Z is above 1 for every gas."]},"10.8":{"s":"A liquid has a fixed volume but takes the shape of its container. Its molecules are close together and move past each other. A liquid evaporates, and in a closed vessel the vapour reaches a steady vapour pressure that rises with temperature. At the boiling point the vapour pressure equals the external pressure. Other properties of liquids are surface tension, which makes drops round, and viscosity, which is the resistance to flow.","w":["The boiling point depends on the external pressure: it is lower on a mountain, higher in a pressure cooker.","Surface tension and viscosity both fall as the temperature rises."]}};
